/Title (The figure below distills the rest of Part 3 into a single flowchart. First ask what type the data is: numeric, binary, or categorical. For numeric data, ask whether it is dense or sparse; dense, uncorrelated data defaults to Euclidean distance, while dense data with correlated attributes calls for Mahalanobis distance, and sparse numeric data \(ratings, embeddings, documents\) calls for cosine similarity. Binary data uses Jaccard \(for sparse, mostly-absent attributes\); categorical data uses Hamming distance \(a mismatch count\). Every measure introduced in the rest of this Part sits somewhere on this tree. )
/Dest [15 0 R /XYZ 72.080566 187.67383 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
/SE 56 0 R
/Prev 756 0 R
/Next 760 0 R
>>
