/Title (Document length shows the mirror image. Let y be a review, y-doubled be the exact same review pasted twice \(double length, identical term proportions\), and y-different be a different, longer review with many new terms. Cosine correctly treats y and y-doubled as near-identical, because doubling every term count is a pure scaling operation; Euclidean distance does not, because it is sensitive to the raw magnitude of the vectors. )
/Dest [31 0 R /XYZ 72.177246 254.19678 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
/SE 224 0 R
/Prev 803 0 R
/Next 799 0 R
>>
endobj
802 0 obj
<<
/Title (A distance function is called a metric when it satisfies three axioms. This matters because many algorithms - k-nearest-neighbours, k-means, and others - implicitly assume the proximity measure they are given behaves like a metric. )
/Dest [18 0 R /XYZ 72.187988 684.97266 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
/SE 81 0 R
/Prev 800 0 R
/Next 804 0 R
>>
endobj
