897 0 obj
<<
/Title (Covariance and correlation are two related but different numbers, and it is easy to conflate them. )
/Dest [26 0 R /XYZ 72.520996 246.99805 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
/SE 172 0 R
/Prev 899 0 R
/Next 895 0 R
>>
endobj
898 0 obj
<<
/Title (Euclidean distance is the default distance almost everyone reaches for first, and under controlled, consistent conditions, it works well - for example, comparing two 128-dimensional face embeddings correctly separates "same person" from "different person" when lighting and pose are held fixed. )
/Dest [22 0 R /XYZ 72.483398 642.11719 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
/SE 134 0 R
/Prev 896 0 R
/Next 900 0 R
>>
endobj
899 0 obj
<<
/Title (4.3 Correlation vs. Covariance )
/Dest [26 0 R /XYZ 72.228516 278.24219 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
/SE 171 0 R
/Prev 901 0 R
/Next 897 0 R
>>
endobj
900 0 obj
<<
/Title (The failure mode appears when conditions change. Under a lighting or pose change, some embedding dimensions shift far more than others, and Euclidean distance treats every dimension as equally important - so it gets fooled. A concrete illustration with toy 4-dimensional embeddings \(in reality 128-dimensional\) makes this precise: Person A photographed under normal light and Person A photographed under different light should be recognized as the same person, and Person B is a genuinely different person. )
/Dest [22 0 R /XYZ 72.080566 585.83398 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
/SE 135 0 R
/Prev 898 0 R
/Next 902 0 R
>>
endobj
901 0 obj
<<
/Title (If correlation is 0, there is no linear relationship, but a non-linear one can still exist, and can even be perfectly deterministic. A measure that is not blind to curves is needed, which motivates mutual information later in this Part. )
/Dest [26 0 R /XYZ 72.714355 335.15137 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
/SE 170 0 R
/Prev 903 0 R
/Next 899 0 R
>>
endobj
